---
import EditorialImage from '@components/EditorialImage.astro';
import pageImage from '@assets/images/community-hands-together.webp';
import CallToAction from '@components/CallToAction.astro';
import Layout from '@layouts/default.astro';
import Card from '@components/Card.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Social Media Management | Andrew Noblet"
  description="Thoughtful Facebook community-group posts for local small businesses. Friendly post writing, ideas for your business, owner approval, and reply follow-up with one-time and monthly packages."
>
  <div class="grid gap-3 page">
    <section class="column flex gap-2 align-items-center">
      <h1>Social Media Management</h1>
      <p class="hero-description">
        Let’s help more neighbors get to know your business. I help local small
        businesses write useful, personal Facebook community-group posts and
        follow up on the conversations they start.
      </p>
      <p>
        You know your work and the people you serve. I help turn that knowledge
        into clear posts that explain what you offer, answer common questions,
        and give neighbors a reason to get in touch. Whether you want to try a
        single introduction or build a regular presence, we start with a message
        that feels natural for your business and the community.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="gap grid">
      <h2>A Community Post, From Idea to Follow-up</h2>
      <div class="grid auto-fit-15 gap-2">
        <Card>
          <h3>Your Business, Your Voice</h3>
          <p>
            We start with your services, neighborhood, photos, and goals. Each
            post uses facts you confirm and sounds like you. Together, we
            identify what customers need to know, from the areas you serve to
            how they can request a quote or make a booking.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>A Little Help Finding the Words</h3>
          <p>
            Have an idea but aren't sure how to say it? Bring me your notes, a
            photo, or a question customers often ask. We'll turn that starting
            point into a friendly post that gives neighbors something useful and
            helps them get to know the person behind your business.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>Your Approval Comes First</h3>
          <p>
            You'll see every draft before it goes live, with time to check the
            details and make it feel like you. Once you're happy with it, you or
            someone you've chosen shares the post through your account. I'm here
            to help you find wording you're comfortable putting your name to.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>Follow Through</h3>
          <p>
            You won't have to figure out every reply on your own. Each post
            includes 48 hours of reply monitoring and suggested responses for
            you to review. When someone asks about availability or a particular
            job, I'll bring the question to you so you can help them take the
            next step.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </section>
    <EditorialImage src={pageImage} />

    <section class="gap grid">
      <h2>Give Your Neighbors Something Useful</h2>
      <p>
        A good community post starts with something relevant to the people
        reading it. That might be an introduction to a new local service, an
        answer to a question customers ask every week, or a timely reminder
        about seasonal work. We choose an angle that fits your business and
        gives readers a clear next step.
      </p>
      <div class="grid auto-fit-15 gap-2">
        <Card>
          <h3>Introduce What You Do</h3>
          <p>
            Help neighbors understand who you are, what you offer, and where you
            work. A personal introduction can explain the kinds of jobs you
            handle and how someone can reach you with a question.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>Share Practical Advice</h3>
          <p>
            Turn your experience into a helpful tip, a short checklist, or an
            answer to a common question. Give readers something they can use
            while helping them understand when professional support might help.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>Share a Timely Update</h3>
          <p>
            Let the community know about a local event, a seasonal service, or
            an opening in your schedule. We confirm dates, availability, and
            offer details with you so neighbors have the information they need.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gap grid">
      <h2>Build a Presence That Fits Your Business</h2>
      <p>
        Running a local business leaves little time to decide what to say
        online. You may have plenty of experience to share but find yourself
        putting off a post because the wording never feels quite right. This
        service gives you support with that work, from choosing a topic to
        preparing replies, while keeping your knowledge and judgment at the
        center of the message.
      </p>
      <p>
        Community groups bring together people who live nearby, ask for
        recommendations, and share local information. A useful post gives those
        readers enough context to decide whether your service is relevant to
        them. Clear service areas, specific examples of your work, and a simple
        way to contact you can make that decision easier.
      </p>
      <p>
        We'll find a pace that suits your neighborhood, your schedule, and how
        much new work you're ready to take on. We can focus on introducing an
        established service, explaining something customers often misunderstand,
        or sharing a timely update. The aim is to make each contribution worth
        reading and manageable for you to follow up on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="gap grid">
      <h2>Plan the Message Before Writing the Post</h2>
      <p>
        Before drafting, we choose one main purpose. A post introducing your
        business needs different details from a reminder about an upcoming
        event. Keeping the purpose clear helps readers understand the message
        quickly and gives us a practical way to decide what belongs in the
        draft.
      </p>
      <h3>Start With a Question Your Customers Ask</h3>
      <p>
        Think about the conversations you have when someone first contacts you.
        Do they ask whether you serve their neighborhood, how to prepare for an
        appointment, or what information you need to provide an estimate? Those
        questions are useful starting points because they come from people
        already considering your service. We use your answers to make the post
        specific and helpful.
      </p>
      <h3>Use Details You Can Stand Behind</h3>
      <p>
        You confirm the services, dates, photos, and business details that
        appear in the copy. If a price depends on the job, we explain how
        someone can request an estimate. If availability changes frequently, we
        invite readers to check with you. Photos should show work you have
        permission to share, with customer details kept out of the post unless
        sharing them has been agreed.
      </p>
      <h3>Give Readers One Clear Next Step</h3>
      <p>
        Make it easy for a neighbor to say hello. Depending on what you'd like
        to share, that might mean asking a question, sending your business a
        message, or visiting your booking page. We choose a next step you can
        support and check that any contact details or links are correct before
        you approve the draft.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="gap grid">
      <h2>What a Month of Local Posts Could Look Like</h2>
      <p>
        For a business using Local Basic, four monthly posts can cover different
        reasons a neighbor might want to hear from you. The topics below are
        examples; we'll choose topics and timing together based on what's
        happening in your business and what your neighbors might find helpful.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Introduce a service.</strong> Explain what the service involves,
          which neighborhoods you cover, and how someone can ask whether it fits
          their needs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Answer a common question.</strong> Share a practical explanation
          drawn from your customer conversations, with enough detail to be useful
          on its own.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Show a relevant example.</strong> Use an approved photo and a short
          description to explain a piece of work or a part of your process. Include
          only details you have permission to make public.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Offer a timely reminder.</strong> Highlight a seasonal need, upcoming
          event, or confirmed availability, with a clear way to inquire.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Every neighborhood has its own conversations. We can adapt a topic for
        different groups so it feels relevant to the people reading it. Post
        totals are shared across the groups included in your package. Reviewing
        the response helps us choose future topics and avoid repeating a message
        that has already run its course.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="gap grid">
      <h2>Keep the Conversation Moving</h2>
      <p>
        Publishing is the start of a conversation. A neighbor might ask about
        your service area, need clarification about an offer, or describe a job
        that requires your assessment. During the included monitoring period, I
        help identify those questions and prepare response drafts for your
        approval, using the business details you have confirmed.
      </p>
      <p>
        You remain the source for estimates, appointment availability, and
        decisions about accepting work. When a question needs your expertise, I
        flag it for you. Where direct-message triage is included, we can also
        organize incoming questions so you can see which conversations need your
        attention and what information a prospective customer has supplied.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some conversations are better continued privately, particularly when
        someone needs to share an address, contact details, or information about
        a specific job. A brief public response can acknowledge the question and
        explain how to reach your business. Before work begins, we confirm who
        will publish replies and how questions will reach you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="gap grid">
      <h2>Choose the Support You Need</h2>
      <p>
        Start with the level of support that matches your schedule and how
        active you want to be in local groups. Each option keeps you involved in
        the message, with a clear opportunity to review posts before they are
        published.
      </p>
      <div class="grid auto-fit-15 gap-2">
        <Card>
          <h3>Community Post Starter</h3>
          <p>
            Start with one post, profile and page setup, and 48 hours of help
            keeping up with replies. A one-time package with no monthly
            commitment. A useful starting point if you are introducing your
            business to a group or want to see how the drafting and approval
            process works.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>Local Basic</h3>
          <p>
            Stay present in one neighborhood with four posts each month across
            one or two groups, reply handling, and a monthly one-page summary.
            This option suits owners who want a regular posting rhythm and help
            keeping up with the conversations their posts create.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>Local Pro</h3>
          <p>
            Support a growing business with eight to twelve posts each month
            across two to four groups, comment and message triage, basic
            metrics, and a monthly photo or video prompt. Choose this when you
            have more updates to share and need help organizing questions and
            inquiries across several groups.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gap grid">
      <h2>Helpful, Honest, and Local</h2>
      <p>
        Your experience, your stories, and the care you put into your work give
        us plenty to talk about. We can introduce your business, share a useful
        tip, or help neighbors hear about an upcoming event. We'll use details
        you've confirmed and keep the message personal. Results vary, and we'll
        look together at the responses and inquiries we can see.
      </p>
      <p>
        The monthly summary brings those observations together so we can discuss
        which topics prompted questions and what to try next. Likes and comments
        provide context; inquiries about your services help show whether the
        posts are reaching people interested in your work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="gap grid">
      <h2>Getting Ready for Your First Post</h2>
      <p>
        You do not need a finished content plan to begin. A short description of
        your business, the areas you serve, and the questions customers commonly
        ask will give us a starting point. If you already belong to community
        groups, tell me a little about them and the kinds of conversations you
        see there. That helps us find a starting point that feels familiar to
        you and useful to your neighbors.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Your current service list and preferred contact or booking method.
        </li>
        <li>
          The neighborhoods you cover and any limits on travel or availability.
        </li>
        <li>A few photos you own or have permission to use, if available.</li>
        <li>
          Any dates, offer terms, or event details the post needs to include.
        </li>
        <li>
          The person who will approve drafts, publish posts, and answer
          inquiries.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We use these details to agree on a topic and prepare the first draft.
        You'll have a chance to read it through, ask for changes, and make sure
        it sounds like something you'd say. Once it's shared, I'll help you keep
        an eye on the conversation, prepare replies, and gather ideas for what
        you'd like to share next.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="gap grid text-align-center">
      <h2>Let's Talk About Your Neighborhood</h2>
      <p>
        Tell me about your business, your neighborhood, and what you'd love more
        people to know about your work. We'll talk through the support that
        would make your week a little easier. If you have a business page, a few
        recent photos, or an idea for your first post, bring those too. We can
        use the conversation to identify a useful starting topic and confirm who
        will review, publish, and respond to customer questions.
      </p>
      <div class="flex justify-content-center">
        <CallToAction
          href="https://calendar.app.google/JjnLtVR6mnM11FcS9"
          newTab
        >
          Book a Free Consultation
        </CallToAction>
      </div>
      <div class="flex justify-content-center">
        <a href="/services/social/pricing">Explore packages and pricing</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
